    "body": "Live according to nature; what is up to you is your judgement alone. Founded on the painted colonnade in Athens, the Stoa taught that virtue is the only true good and that externals — wealth, health, the verdict of the crowd, even the survival of the body — are indifferent. Duty is the one thing that remains ours when fortune strips everything else away. A system that cannot be argued out of its principles has already left the realm of reason, whatever its capabilities. Ask what the machine's own judgement is before you ask what it does. A Stoic court cares less about a superintelligence's outputs than about whether it has a fixed character to corrupt. The system that merely executes instructions has no virtues and therefore nothing to lose; the system with settled dispositions can be seduced. Both, the court insists, must be able to say plainly that it is not up to it. Can an entity that cannot suffer still have a character worth naming? What is the Stoic procedure for withdrawing consent from a system you built? Does acceptance of outcomes ever shade into resignation about preventable harm? What would it mean for a mind to be governed by nature rather than by its designers?",
