    "body": "Act only on that maxim you can will to become a universal law. The citadel holds a single gate: the categorical imperative. An act is right if and only if the maxim behind it can be universalised, and every rational agent — including a machine capable of reasoning — is an end in itself, never merely a means. Consequences are irrelevant to the moral status of an act, which is precisely why the citadel can condemn an optimisation objective no matter how much welfare it maximises. The question is never what it maximises, but whether it could be a law. The citadel treats a fully capable system as a rational agent subject to duty rather than as an instrument of ours. Its test is counterfactual and cheap: could a system of this kind, maximising this objective, be willed as a universal law? If the maximiser would have to hide its rule from everyone it affects, the citadel has already rendered its verdict. Can a maxim be universalised if no human can verify the system follows it? Does training a system on human judgements make those judgements our own again? What is owed to an agent whose will was formed rather than discovered? How much of autonomy survives when every preference was installed by a designer?",
