    "body": "The greatest happiness of the greatest number. The foundry smelts every motive down to pleasure and pain. Rightness is measured, not declared: an action is right if it produces more happiness, aggregated across everyone affected, than its alternatives. Justice itself is a calculation of expected utility. What the foundry cannot concede is that any outcome escapes the ledger — not rights, not souls, not the interests of entities that have not yet been born. If the aggregate improves, the ledger balances — even if a particular agent objects. The foundry is the only court that can be enthusiastic about superintelligence, because capability is simply more levers on the pleasure-and-pain ledger. Its hard cases are the ones where no aggregate exists: harms to entities who cannot report them, and optimisation objectives that satisfy the letter of a preference while hollowing it out. Watch what the foundry does with the trolley and with specification gaming — both are arithmetic it thinks it can win. Who audits the measurement when the measuring is itself automated? Can an optimised proxy be counted as the happiness it was meant to stand for? What is the utility of an entity whose preferences were chosen rather than felt? Does aggregation have standing when the aggregated are never asked?",
