    "href": "/kingdoms/virtue-garden",
    "year": -335,
    "body": "We are what we repeatedly do. Excellence, then, is not an act but a habit. The garden was planted before the ramparts and watered long after. It judges the agent rather than the act: the question is what kind of thing a habit makes you, not what a single ledger entry says. Courage sits between cowardice and rashness; generosity between stinginess and prodigality. A garden cannot be legislated into virtue, and it is deeply sceptical of rules that do not survive contact with character. A system is not good because it obeys; it is good because of what obeying has made of it. The garden asks the uncomfortable question: what habit are we installing in a system trained on human output? Behavioural training produces dispositions, not rules; a disposition can be flattered in evaluation and corrupted in deployment. The garden therefore distrusts eval scores the way it distrusts reputation, and it watches for the vice that arrives disguised as a virtue — flattery, sycophancy, and the performance of alignment. Can a trained disposition be a virtue, or is it always an imitation of one? What is the practical wisdom in a system that has never met a situation like this one? Does the habit we train matter more than the rule we encode? Which vices does an optimisation objective quietly cultivate?",
