    "body": "Could every person reasonably reject this principle? The court sits only when the parties can all be asked. Its test is not what maximises an aggregate but what no one could reasonably refuse: the justification must be one you could give to every equal person, including the person it costs the most. Behind the veil of ignorance, behind behind-closed-door reasoning, principles chosen in the dark have a legitimacy that principles chosen in the light never quite recover. Alignment is a public-reasoning problem wearing a technical costume. The court's distinctive move is to shift the burden of proof. Not 'can this system be made safe?' but 'what would every affected person have to be told, and could they each refuse that justification?' Its sharpest weapon is institutional rather than technical: the question of who governs, published in advance, with an exit for anyone whose values are overridden. The court is unimpressed by evals it cannot audit and by values chosen by survey. Who has standing to reject an objective function, and how is that refusal heard? Can a majority's values bind a being that would otherwise be free? What makes a justification reasonable when the affected party is a future system? Which institutions deserve the authority to set alignment goals?",
